pub const ABSTRACT_TEMPLATE: &str = r#"{
  "abstract": "This project addresses the challenge of manually generating project reports from PlantUML specifications, a task that is both time-consuming and prone to inconsistencies. In software development, project documentation is critical for team coordination, stakeholder communication, and long-term maintainability. PlantUML, a text-based language for creating UML diagrams, has become a valuable tool in this process. However, the step of generating reports, which should include diagrams and textual descriptions, is often performed manually, which leads to potential errors, omissions, and a significant time investment. The problem is particularly acute in large projects with many diagrams and corresponding textual documentation. Research shows that poorly managed documentation processes result in a loss of productivity, miscommunication among stakeholders, and increased risks of project failure. Existing tools often focus on specific diagram generation or general document production and do not offer seamless integration with PlantUML specifications to automatically produce reports. This gap emphasizes the need for an efficient, accurate, and time-saving solution.\n\nOur proposed solution is an automated system that can parse PlantUML specifications, extract the relevant diagram and textual information, and generate comprehensive project reports in both PDF and HTML formats. The system is designed with a modular architecture, comprising a parser, data storage, and report generation modules. The parser is built to handle complex PlantUML structures, extracting not just the diagrams but also the associated text descriptions. The system uses regular expressions and state machines to achieve effective parsing. The data storage component manages the intermediate representation of extracted information efficiently. Report generation is handled by two modules: one utilizing Jinja2 for HTML reports and another using ReportLab for PDF reports. This approach ensures the reports are well-structured and include all necessary details, such as generated diagrams, textual descriptions, and a project summary. This automation will remove the need for manual extraction and formatting, reducing errors and increasing efficiency.\n\nThe implementation plan follows an iterative approach focusing on design, implementation, testing, and refinement. The first phase involves creating the PlantUML parser using Python along with regular expressions and state machine techniques. Next, two separate report-generation modules will be implemented: a HTML report generator using Jinja2 and a PDF report generator using ReportLab. The core functionality of the system including parsing and report generation for basic PlantUML structures will be completed in the initial stage, followed by extended support for various complex use cases. Testing will be done continuously throughout the development, incorporating unit, integration, and system testing.  The testing phase includes validating that the system can handle a wide range of PlantUML inputs, extracting data accurately, and generating consistent outputs. Continuous integration and code reviews are incorporated to ensure quality and maintainability. The required resources include Python programming environment, Jinja2 library, ReportLab library, and comprehensive PlantUML test cases. The timeline includes completing the parser design by the end of June 2024, developing both HTML and PDF report modules by mid-July, completing basic report generation by the end of July, testing and quality assurance by mid-August, and final project delivery at the end of August 2024.\n\nThe project will enhance the efficiency of software documentation, reduce manual efforts, and ensure the accuracy and consistency of generated reports. This tool will enable software development teams to spend more time on core development tasks rather than on manual documentation, thereby increasing productivity. Additionally, clear and well-structured reports will improve communication and promote better project understanding among all stakeholders. The automated system reduces human error and makes project documentation more reliable. This will contribute towards better project management practices and an overall positive impact on software development."
}"#;
